1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:03,320
Every time your browser or your app sends

2
00:00:03,330 --> 00:00:06,020
a request to a server, the server sends

3
00:00:06,030 --> 00:00:07,100
back a response

4
00:00:07,110 --> 00:00:09,572
And the first line of that response has a

5
00:00:09,582 --> 00:00:12,190
three-digit number. The number is the HTTP

6
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:14,700
status code. In this case here, 200 is the

7
00:00:14,710 --> 00:00:16,910
status code. OK is the reason phrase

8
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:18,950
The job of the status code is to tell you

9
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,820
what happened while the body gives you the

10
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:23,310
detail. And you can already tell a lot

11
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,900
from the first digit of the number

12
00:00:25,910 --> 00:00:28,510
HTTP status codes are grouped into five

13
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:30,420
classes. The first digit of the three

14
00:00:30,430 --> 00:00:32,590
digit number tells you which class it is

15
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,230
The easiest way to think of them is to

16
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:37,380
think of asking for a book at a bookshop

17
00:00:37,390 --> 00:00:40,550
counter. 1xx means, hold on, I'm working

18
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:41,970
on it. Like the attendant telling you,

19
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:43,550
hey, give me a second, let me check

20
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:45,540
2xx means success

21
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:46,500
I've got your book, right?

22
00:00:46,510 --> 00:00:47,580
Yeah, it is

23
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:49,740
And 3xx means redirection

24
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:52,470
We moved that book to our other branch, go

25
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:54,480
there. 4xx means client error

26
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:56,220
Something is wrong with what you asked

27
00:00:56,230 --> 00:00:57,680
for. Maybe you asked for a book that

28
00:00:57,690 --> 00:00:59,130
doesn't exist or you didn't show your

29
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:00,400
Bookshop membership card

30
00:01:00,410 --> 00:01:02,000
5xx means server error

31
00:01:02,010 --> 00:01:04,040
Your request was fine, but something broke

32
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:04,680
on our end

33
00:01:04,690 --> 00:01:06,720
Our bookstore computer system went down,

34
00:01:06,730 --> 00:01:07,740
whatever it is

35
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:10,050
So when something fails or succeeds, look

36
00:01:10,060 --> 00:01:11,880
at the first digit of this three-digit

37
00:01:11,890 --> 00:01:14,160
number first. If we start with a four, we

38
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:15,760
start by checking the request the client

39
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:17,600
sends. If we start with a five, we start

40
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:18,640
by checking the server

41
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:20,210
Now we'll go through each of them, each of

42
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:22,030
this group using our bookstore API

43
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:24,790
The 1xx status codes, you rarely see this

44
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:27,367
for yourself. You might see 101 Switching

45
00:01:27,377 --> 00:01:30,080
Protocols when a connection upgrades to a

46
00:01:30,090 --> 00:01:32,550
WebSocket, but your day-to-day API is

47
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:36,280
mostly 2xx to 5xx HTTP status codes

48
00:01:36,290 --> 00:01:39,080
The 2xx status codes mean success

49
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:41,640
And there are three that you will use all

50
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:42,080
of the time

51
00:01:42,090 --> 00:01:44,320
200 OK means your request worked

52
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:45,280
Here's the result

53
00:01:45,290 --> 00:01:48,440
So GET book 42 returns 200 OK with the

54
00:01:48,450 --> 00:01:51,020
book. 201 Created means the request worked

55
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:52,340
and something new was created

56
00:01:52,350 --> 00:01:54,920
So when you POST books to add a new book,

57
00:01:54,930 --> 00:01:56,840
the right answer is 201

58
00:01:56,850 --> 00:01:58,420
You can also add a Location header that

59
00:01:58,430 --> 00:01:59,280
points to the new book

60
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:02,410
204 No Content, means that your request

61
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:04,200
worked, but there's nothing to send back

62
00:02:04,210 --> 00:02:06,640
Usually we get a 204 a lot with delete

63
00:02:06,650 --> 00:02:08,490
For example, in DELETE book 42, there is

64
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:09,520
nothing left to show

65
00:02:09,530 --> 00:02:12,720
So you return 204 with an empty body

66
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:16,131
The 3xx HTTP status codes are redirects

67
00:02:16,141 --> 00:02:20,230
They tell the client that what it wants is

68
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:21,360
at a different

69
00:02:21,370 --> 00:02:23,560
address. So the new address goes in the

70
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:25,520
Location header. There are four common 3xx

71
00:02:25,530 --> 00:02:27,630
HTTP status codes that you will see

72
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:28,830
And to pick the right one, you just need

73
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:29,610
to answer two questions

74
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:31,550
Question one, is the move permanent or

75
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:33,390
temporary? If a bookshop moves that book

76
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,900
to the other branch for good, that's a 301

77
00:02:36,910 --> 00:02:37,930
or a 308

78
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:39,600
If the book is only there for now and it's

79
00:02:39,610 --> 00:02:41,850
coming back, that's a 302 or 307

80
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:44,520
The second question, does the request stay

81
00:02:44,530 --> 00:02:46,070
the same when the client follows the

82
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:48,020
redirect. So you come to the counter to

83
00:02:48,030 --> 00:02:50,020
hand in an order form, and they send it to

84
00:02:50,030 --> 00:02:51,020
the other branch

85
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:53,540
With 307 or 308, you take the same form to

86
00:02:53,550 --> 00:02:55,010
the other branch, and you hand it in

87
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:57,630
there. With 301 and 302, the browser is

88
00:02:57,640 --> 00:02:59,810
allowed to switch from POST to GET

89
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:01,610
So for normal web pages, 301 and 302 are

90
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:03,560
fine. If you're redirecting an API where

91
00:03:03,570 --> 00:03:06,240
POST requests matter, I would use 307 and

92
00:03:06,250 --> 00:03:09,520
308. Now the 4xx status codes are client

93
00:03:09,530 --> 00:03:10,790
errors. They mean the system thinks

94
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:12,660
something is wrong with the request and

95
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:14,430
they are the ones you would debug the

96
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:16,770
most. Let's start with 400 and 422 because

97
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:17,410
they sound similar

98
00:03:17,420 --> 00:03:19,080
400 Bad Request means the server can't

99
00:03:19,090 --> 00:03:20,410
read your request properly

100
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:22,450
Like broken JSON with a missing bracket

101
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:24,970
422 Unprocessable Content means that the

102
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:26,530
server could read the request just fine,

103
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:28,210
but the data inside does not make sense

104
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:30,940
If you send a bookstore a new book with no

105
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:33,480
title or something like that, you get 422

106
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:36,110
Some APIs send 400 for validation errors

107
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:38,150
too. I think 422 is clearer because it

108
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:39,810
tells the front end that the format was

109
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:41,970
fine and data is what needs fixing

110
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:43,790
404 Not Found means that there's nothing

111
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:44,370
in the address

112
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:47,300
So if I send GET book 9999 and there's no

113
00:03:47,310 --> 00:03:49,010
book 9999, the book does not exist

114
00:03:49,020 --> 00:03:51,370
So this client gets a 404 Not Found

115
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:52,890
You probably send a 404 yourself

116
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:55,770
410 Gone is a stronger version of 404

117
00:03:55,780 --> 00:03:57,600
It means that this resource was here, it

118
00:03:57,610 --> 00:03:59,240
was removed on purpose and it should not

119
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:00,690
be back. Like a book that you've taken out

120
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:02,600
of the catalog for good

121
00:04:02,610 --> 00:04:05,310
In practice, most APIs use 404, but

122
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:08,190
Google's docs say it treats 404 and 410

123
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:08,970
the same way

124
00:04:08,980 --> 00:04:10,910
So you use 410 Gone when you want to be

125
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:12,850
clear that something is gone for good

126
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:15,930
409 Conflict means the request clashed

127
00:04:15,940 --> 00:04:17,720
with the current state of the thing that

128
00:04:17,730 --> 00:04:19,770
you're changing. Say two admins open book

129
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:21,110
42 at the same time

130
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:22,570
Admin A changes the price and save

131
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:23,860
Admin B is still looking at the old

132
00:04:23,870 --> 00:04:26,050
version. They change the title and save

133
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:27,930
And that save will put the old price back

134
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:30,190
So if the API keeps track of versions, It

135
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,240
can reply with 409 Conflict to admin B

136
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:34,060
instead, so that it can reload and try

137
00:04:34,070 --> 00:04:36,190
again. 429, Too Many Requests means you

138
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:37,210
have been rate limited

139
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:39,130
It means you have sent too many requests

140
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:41,060
in a short time and is asking you to slow

141
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:43,410
down. Now, 401 versus 403

142
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:45,950
401 versus 403 are two status codes that

143
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:47,930
people mix up together a lot

144
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:50,070
Say our bookstore has an admin route,

145
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:51,810
DELETE book 42

146
00:04:51,820 --> 00:04:53,290
Only admins can delete book

147
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:54,770
Case one, now let's look at two scenarios

148
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:56,440
Case one, a request comes in with no token

149
00:04:56,450 --> 00:04:57,730
at all, or the token has expired

150
00:04:57,740 --> 00:04:59,665
The server has no idea who's sending this

151
00:04:59,675 --> 00:05:02,260
request. That is a 401 Unauthorized

152
00:05:02,270 --> 00:05:03,940
Case two, the request has a valid token

153
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:05,660
So the server knows exactly who it is

154
00:05:05,670 --> 00:05:07,340
It's a customer, and customers cannot

155
00:05:07,350 --> 00:05:09,140
delete book. That is a 403 Forbidden

156
00:05:09,150 --> 00:05:11,060
So two different cases, right?

157
00:05:11,070 --> 00:05:12,860
It's like when you have a hotel keycard

158
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:14,530
401 is when you don't even have a keycard

159
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:15,900
at all or your card doesn't work

160
00:05:15,910 --> 00:05:17,960
403 is when your card works fine, but it's

161
00:05:17,970 --> 00:05:19,200
for another room and you're trying to

162
00:05:19,210 --> 00:05:19,900
enter another room

163
00:05:19,910 --> 00:05:21,500
So 401 is kind of who are you?

164
00:05:21,510 --> 00:05:23,340
While 403 is kind of, I know who you are,

165
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:24,580
but you can't do this

166
00:05:24,590 --> 00:05:26,120
The part that confuses people mostly is

167
00:05:26,130 --> 00:05:28,130
the name. 401 is called Unauthorized

168
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:29,770
And meanwhile, it's really about

169
00:05:29,780 --> 00:05:32,420
authentication. 403, on the other hand, is

170
00:05:32,430 --> 00:05:33,910
the one that is about permission

171
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:35,850
On a 401, the right move is to send the

172
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:38,070
user to login or refresh their token

173
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:40,270
On a 403, logging in again won't help

174
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:41,950
because the server already knows who they

175
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:43,550
are. You just need to show them a message

176
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:44,900
that they don't have the access to do

177
00:05:44,910 --> 00:05:47,240
this. Sometimes a server will return 404

178
00:05:47,250 --> 00:05:49,440
instead of 403 on purpose just to hide

179
00:05:49,450 --> 00:05:50,710
that something exists

180
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:52,550
For example, GitHub's API does this

181
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:54,150
If you ask for a private repository that

182
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:56,890
you don't have access to, you get a 404 so

183
00:05:56,900 --> 00:05:59,170
that GitHub doesn't confirm to you that

184
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:00,490
the repository exists

185
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:02,190
You shouldn't have access to it, and you

186
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:04,190
shouldn't even know that it exists

187
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:06,320
Now, the 5xx status codes are server

188
00:06:06,330 --> 00:06:08,690
errors. The requests look fine, but the

189
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:10,390
server failed to handle it

190
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:13,210
500 Internal Server Error is the general

191
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:15,110
one. Something unexpected went wrong

192
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:17,910
Usually, it's an exception in your code

193
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:19,110
that nothing caught

194
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:21,530
In most real setups, a request first hits

195
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:24,860
a proxy, like an NGINX or a load balancer,

196
00:06:24,870 --> 00:06:26,910
and the proxy passes it to your app

197
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:30,300
502 Bad Gateway means that the proxy got

198
00:06:30,310 --> 00:06:32,360
an invalid response from your app or

199
00:06:32,370 --> 00:06:34,240
couldn't get a proper response from it at

200
00:06:34,250 --> 00:06:37,190
all. 504 Gateway Timeout means that the

201
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:38,860
proxy waited for your app, and your app

202
00:06:38,870 --> 00:06:40,470
took too long to answer

203
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:42,360
A very slow database query can actually

204
00:06:42,370 --> 00:06:43,270
cause this one

205
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:45,230
503 Service Unavailable means the server

206
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:47,430
cannot handle requests at the moment, and

207
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:49,330
that situation is temporary

208
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:50,670
It might be that the server is overloaded,

209
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:51,850
or maybe the server is down for

210
00:06:51,860 --> 00:06:54,290
maintenance. And there are some common

211
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:56,320
HTTP status code mistakes in API that

212
00:06:56,330 --> 00:06:58,890
people make. The first one, common one, is

213
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:00,590
returning 200 OK for everything

214
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:02,270
If your request fails and your API still

215
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:04,150
returns 200 OK, with something like error

216
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:05,840
or something went wrong inside the body,

217
00:07:05,850 --> 00:07:07,490
your front end only sees 200

218
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:09,160
Your front end sees 200 and is like, OK,

219
00:07:09,170 --> 00:07:10,050
yeah, book saved

220
00:07:10,060 --> 00:07:11,800
Your monitoring also counts it as a

221
00:07:11,810 --> 00:07:14,130
success. So you need to send the actual

222
00:07:14,140 --> 00:07:16,280
status code and then put the details in

223
00:07:16,290 --> 00:07:18,000
the body. The second mistake that people

224
00:07:18,010 --> 00:07:20,190
make with HTTP status code is using 500

225
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:22,400
for things that the clients got wrong

226
00:07:22,410 --> 00:07:24,360
For example, say someone says, GET book

227
00:07:24,370 --> 00:07:26,700
9999, and you use find instead of

228
00:07:26,710 --> 00:07:28,460
findOrFail. So the book comes back as

229
00:07:28,470 --> 00:07:29,900
null, and then your code tries to read the

230
00:07:29,910 --> 00:07:32,280
title from null, and then it crashes, and

231
00:07:32,290 --> 00:07:33,890
the client gets a 500

232
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:36,430
The right answer is a 404, not a 500,

233
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:38,110
because the book does not exist

234
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:39,710
In this scenario, a 500 is the wrong code,

235
00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:41,390
because 500 says that the server is

236
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:42,670
broken. Meanwhile, the server is not

237
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:44,300
broken. That can just set off an alarm for

238
00:07:44,310 --> 00:07:46,870
nothing. The third mistake is mixing 401

239
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:49,760
and 403. If you return 403 when the user's

240
00:07:49,770 --> 00:07:51,620
token has expired, your front end doesn't

241
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:53,460
send them back to the login, which is what

242
00:07:53,470 --> 00:07:54,390
your front end should do

243
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:55,870
So they just, they are stuck

244
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:58,550
The fourth one is leaking stack traces in

245
00:07:58,560 --> 00:07:59,590
your 500 response

246
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:02,280
In Laravel, for example, if APP_DEBUG is

247
00:08:02,290 --> 00:08:05,040
true and something crashes, the JSON error

248
00:08:05,050 --> 00:08:07,340
response includes the exception message,

249
00:08:07,350 --> 00:08:09,790
your file path, the line number, the full

250
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:12,110
stack trace. That's useful for you as the

251
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:14,070
engineer. In production, however, if you

252
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:15,410
show that, you are showing someone your

253
00:08:15,420 --> 00:08:16,840
folder structure and how your code runs

254
00:08:16,850 --> 00:08:19,610
So APP_DEBUG should always be false in

255
00:08:19,620 --> 00:08:22,214
production, with the body of your 500

256
00:08:22,224 --> 00:08:23,800
response just saying Server Error

257
00:08:23,810 --> 00:08:25,400
That's all your users need to know

258
00:08:25,410 --> 00:08:28,210
And one last HTTP status code, just for

259
00:08:28,220 --> 00:08:29,340
the fun of it

260
00:08:29,350 --> 00:08:31,000
418, I'm a teapot

261
00:08:31,010 --> 00:08:32,587
Bye
